ad to a contradiction because he's already red.\n\nAdditionally, looking at point 18: If Bob is round and big, then he's furry. But from point 13, all round things are rough, and from point 16, all rough things are smart. So, if Bob is round, he's rough, and if he's big, he's smart. But Bob is red, and from point 12, all furry things are red, so if he were furry, he would be red, which he already is. So, being round and big doesn't affect his color; he's already red.\n\nAnother point is point 19: If Fiona is red and white, then she's smart. But Fiona is big and furry, so she's red (from point 5 and 10), and if she were white, she'd have to be smart. But since she's already red, being white would make her smart, but that's not relevant to Bob.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Bob is red, and the rules don't allow him to be both red and white. Therefore, Bob is not white, making the statement \"Bob is not white\" true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. Bob is explicitly stated to be red.\n2. The rules do not allow Bob to be both red and white simultaneously.\n3. Therefore, Bob cannot be white.\n4. Hence, the statement \"Bob is not white\" is true.\n\nAnswer: A) True",
