s. So that's consistent.\n\nNow, going back to Bob. Bob is quiet and white. From point 11, white things are round. So Bob, being white, must be round. But from point 14, all round things are young. Therefore, Bob, being round, must be young.\n\nWait, but the question is whether Bob is not young. If Bob is young, then the statement \"Bob is not young\" would be false. But according to the reasoning, Bob is young because he's white, which makes him round, and being round makes him young.\n\nBut let me double-check. Bob is quiet and white. White things are round, so Bob is round. All round things are young, so Bob is young. Therefore, Bob is young, which means the statement \"Bob is not young\" is false.\n\nWait, but I'm a bit confused because sometimes these puzzles can have hidden assumptions. Let me make sure I didn't miss anything.\n\nBob is quiet and white. White things are round, so Bob is round. All round things are young, so Bob is young. Therefore, Bob is young, so the statement \"Bob is not young\" is false.\n\nYes, that seems correct.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B) False\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. Bob is quiet and white.\n2. White things are round, so Bob is round.\n3. All round things are young, so Bob is young.\n4. Therefore, Bob is young, making the statement \"Bob is not young\" false.\n\nAnswer: B) False",
