ply directly.\n\nStatement 18 says if something is blue, then it likes the rabbit. But I don't know if the bald eagle is blue yet.\n\nStatement 12 says if something likes the bald eagle, then it is blue. So, if the rabbit liked the bald eagle, then the rabbit would be blue. But statement 18 would then mean the rabbit likes the rabbit, which seems a bit circular and not necessarily helpful.\n\nWait, but the question is about the bald eagle liking the rabbit. So, is there any statement that directly or indirectly connects the bald eagle liking the rabbit?\n\nLooking back, statement 3 is the bald eagle seeing the rabbit, but that doesn't necessarily mean it likes the rabbit. Statement 11 only tells us that if the bald eagle sees the rabbit, the rabbit is big, but it doesn't say anything about the bald eagle liking the rabbit.\n\nI don't see any statement that directly links the bald eagle liking the rabbit. The only connection is through seeing, but seeing doesn't equate to liking. So, based on the given information, there's no direct evidence that the bald eagle likes the rabbit.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"The bald eagle likes the rabbit\" is unknown because there's no information provided that links the bald eagle liking the rabbit.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. The bald eagle sees the rabbit (statement 3).\n2. If the bald eagle sees the rabbit, the rabbit is big (statement 11).\n3. The rabbit sees the bald eagle (statement 9).\n4. If the rabbit sees the bald eagle, the rabbit visits the bald eagle (statement 17).\n5. The rabbit is big (from step 2).\n6. However, there is no direct or indirect evidence that the bald eagle likes the rabbit.\n7. Statements about liking are conditional and require more information not provided.\n8. Therefore, the statement is unknown.\n\nAnswer: C) Unknown",
