eds the lion through the mouse.\n\nSo, the rabbit does need the lion because it needs the mouse, which needs the lion. Therefore, the statement \"The rabbit does not need the lion\" would be false.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. Is there any way the rabbit doesn't need the lion? Maybe if the rabbit is big enough that it doesn't need the mouse, but statement 11 says the rabbit needs the mouse regardless. So, the rabbit needs the mouse, which needs the lion. Therefore, the rabbit must need the lion.\n\nSo, the statement \"The rabbit does not need the lion\" is false.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B) False\n\nStep-by-Step Explanation:\n\n1. The rabbit eats the bald eagle (statement 10).\n2. Eating the bald eagle makes the rabbit cold (statement 14).\n3. The rabbit needs the mouse (statement 11).\n4. The mouse needs the lion (statement 8).\n5. Therefore, the rabbit indirectly needs the lion through the mouse.\n6. There's no information suggesting the rabbit doesn't need the lion.\n7. Thus, the statement \"The rabbit does not need the lion\" is false.\n\nAnswer: B) False",
