mouse (indirectly through the dog), but there's no direct fact that the bear sees the mouse. The bear's seeing the dog doesn't imply it sees the mouse unless there's a rule about seeing transitivity, which isn't given.\n\nTherefore, based on the given information, there's no direct evidence that the bear sees the mouse. So the statement \"The bear sees the mouse\" is unknown because we don't have enough information to confirm it.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C) Unknown\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. The bear is green and sees the dog (Fact 20).\n2. The dog likes the mouse (Fact 12) and visits the mouse (Fact 13).\n3. The mouse is cold (Fact 15) and likes the cat (Fact 17).\n4. The cat sees the bear (Fact 6) and the dog (Fact 7).\n5. The bear likes the dog (Fact 3) and the mouse (indirectly through the dog).\n6. There's no direct fact stating the bear sees the mouse.\n7. Without a rule connecting seeing the dog to seeing the mouse, the statement is unknown.\n\nAnswer: C) Unknown",
