e we don't know if the cow is cold.\n\nNext, statement 11: If something visits the cow and it sees the cow, then it visits the bald eagle. So, if the cow sees itself, it would visit the bald eagle. But we don't know if the cow sees itself. The cow sees the rabbit (statement 6), but that's the rabbit, not the cow.\n\nWait, statement 6 says the cow sees the rabbit. So, the cow sees the rabbit, but statement 11 is about something visiting the cow and seeing the cow. So, if the cow sees itself, then it would visit the bald eagle. But we don't have any information about the cow seeing itself. So, this might not be relevant here.\n\nNow, looking at the cat. The cat visits the bald eagle (statement 3) and the rabbit (statement 4). The cat also visits the rabbit (statement 10). The cat is not mentioned to see anything else, but the rabbit sees the cow (statement 6), and the rabbit visits the cat (statement 10). \n\nWait, statement 14 says if something is cold and visits the cow, then the cow sees the cat. The rabbit is cold (statement 8), and the rabbit visits the cow (statement 6). So, does the rabbit visiting the cow and being cold make the cow see the cat? Let me check statement 14 again: \"If something is cold and it visits the cow then the cow sees the cat.\" So, the antecedent is \"something is cold and it visits the cow.\" The rabbit is cold and visits the cow, so according to statement 14, the cow sees the cat.\n\nWait, but statement 6 says the cow sees the rabbit, not the cat. So, if statement 14 is true, then the cow would see the cat as well. But the question is whether the cow does not see the cat. So, if the cow sees the cat, then the statement \"cow does not see cat\" would be false. But if the cow doesn't see the cat, then it's true.\n\nBut wait, let me make sure. Statement 14 is a conditional: if something is cold and visits the cow, then the cow sees the cat. So, the antecedent is \"something is cold and visits the cow.\" The rabbit is cold and visits the cow (statement 6). Therefore, the conclusion is that the cow sees the cat.\n\nBut wait, statement 6 says the cow sees the rabbit, not the cat. So, does the cow see the cat because of statement 14? Or is there a contradiction?\n\nWait, statement 14 is a conditional. It doesn't say that the cow must see the cat; it just says that if the antecedent is true, then the cow sees the cat. So, if the antecedent is true, then the cow sees the cat. But does the antecedent hold?\n\nThe antecedent is \"something is cold and it visits the cow.\" The rabbit is cold and visits the cow (statement 6). So, the antecedent is true, which would make the conclusion that the cow sees the cat true.\n\nBut wait, statement 6 says the cow sees the rabbit, not the cat. So, does the cow see the cat because of statement 14, or is there a conflict?\n\nI think statement 14 is a rule that applies whenever the antecedent is true. So, if the antecedent is true, then the cow sees the cat. Therefore, the cow sees the cat.\n\nBut wait, statement 6 says the cow sees the rabbit, which is a different entity. So, does the cow see the cat as well? Or is there a conflict?\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let's see: The antecedent is \"something is cold and it visits the cow.\" The rabbit is cold and visits the cow, so the antecedent is true. Therefore, the cow sees the cat. So, the cow sees the cat.\n\nBut the question is whether the cow does not see the cat. So, if the cow does see the cat, then the statement \"cow does not see cat\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any other information that might contradict this.\n\nStatement 14 is a conditional, so it doesn't necessarily mean that the cow must see the cat; it just says that if the antecedent is true, then the cow sees the cat. So, if the antecedent is true, the cow sees the cat. Therefore, the cow does see the cat.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"cow does not see cat\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. The antecedent is \"something is