 is green, then they are white. So, Anne is green, so she must be white. So, Anne is green and white.\n\nNow, statement 13: If someone is blue and white, then they are kind. But Anne is blue (from statement 8) and white (from above), so she must be kind.\n\nBut wait, statement 15 says if someone is young and not white, then they are rough. Anne is young and white, so this condition doesn't apply. So, we can't conclude that she's rough based on that.\n\nWait, but statement 5 says Gary is not rough. So, Gary is not rough. But we don't have any information about Anne's roughness yet.\n\nWait, let me check statement 14: All red people are not rough. Since Anne is red (from earlier), that means she is not rough. So, Anne is not rough.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't miss anything. Let me go through each step again.\n\n1. Anne is young (statement 1).\n2. All young people are blue (statement 8), so Anne is blue.\n3. Blue, young people are red (statement 11), so Anne is red.\n4. All red people are green (statement 10), so Anne is green.\n5. If someone is green, then they are white (statement 12), so Anne is white.\n6. If someone is blue and white, then they are kind (statement 13), so Anne is kind.\n7. All red people are not rough (statement 14), so Anne is not rough.\n\nTherefore, Anne is not rough, so the statement \"Anne is rough\" is false.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B) False\n\nStep-by-Step Explanation:\n\n1. **Anne is young** (given).\n2. **All young people are blue** (statement 8), so Anne is blue.\n3. **Blue, young people are red** (statement 11), so Anne is red.\n4. **All red people are green** (statement 10), so Anne is green.\n5. **If someone is green, then they are white** (statement 12), so Anne is white.\n6. **If someone is blue and white, then they are kind** (statement 13), so Anne is kind.\n7. **All red people are not rough** (statement 14), so Anne is not rough.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Anne is rough\" is false.\n\nThe correct option is: B) False",
