e the bear. So, the bear doesn't like the lion, so statement 19 doesn't apply. Therefore, the bear doesn't necessarily like the lion, so we can't conclude the lion likes the dog.\n\nSince the lion doesn't like the bear (statement 12), and the bear doesn't necessarily like the lion, we can't say the lion likes the dog. Therefore, the lion doesn't like the dog, so statement 17 doesn't apply, meaning the lion doesn't like the bald eagle.\n\nWait, but statement 3 says the bald eagle likes the lion. Does that mean the lion likes the bald eagle? Not necessarily. Liking is not necessarily mutual unless there's a rule that says so.\n\nLooking back, statement 17 says if something likes the dog, then it likes the bald eagle. But the lion doesn't like the dog, so that doesn't help. So, the lion doesn't like the dog, so it doesn't like the bald eagle.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way. Let me check all the rules again.\n\nIs there any rule that directly connects the lion liking the bald eagle? Statement 3 says the bald eagle likes the lion, but that's one-way. There's no rule that says if the bald eagle likes the lion, then the lion likes the bald eagle. So, it's possible, but not necessarily true.\n\nWait, but maybe through other statements. Let's see.\n\nThe bear needs the dog (statement 8). The dog is red (statement 10). The dog does not need the bear (statement 11). The dog chases the bald eagle (statement 9). The dog is red (statement 10). The dog does not need the bear (statement 11). The bear is not blue (statement 6). The bear is nice (statement 7). The bear needs the dog (statement 8). The lion does not like the bear (statement 12). If something likes the lion and is not red, it chases the lion (statement 13). The bald eagle likes the lion (statement 3). The bald eagle is blue (statement 1) and red (statement 2). Wait, that's contradictory. The bald eagle can't be both blue and red. That's confusing. Maybe that's a mistake, but perhaps it's a trick. Maybe it'