iger is green, so it must eat the rabbit. But point 14 says the tiger eats the dog. So does that mean the tiger is eating both the dog and the rabbit? Or is there a rule that says a tiger can't eat two things, which isn't stated.\n\nWait, maybe the tiger can eat both, but the fact that it's eating the dog doesn't affect its eating the rabbit. So the tiger is green, so it must eat the rabbit, making the statement \"The tiger needs the rabbit\" true.\n\nBut wait, point 20 says if the tiger needs the rabbit, then the rabbit is green. But the rabbit's color isn't given. So if the tiger needs the rabbit, the rabbit must be green. But we don't know if the rabbit is green or not. So maybe the rabbit isn't green, which would mean the tiger doesn't need the rabbit.\n\nHmm, this is getting confusing. Let me try to break it down.\n\n1. Tiger is green (point 15).\n2. If someone is green, they eat the rabbit (point 17). So tiger must eat the rabbit.\n3. Therefore, the tiger needs the rabbit because it's eating it.\n\nBut wait, point 19 says if someone eats the rabbit, they need the squirrel. So the tiger is eating the rabbit, so it needs the squirrel. But the question is about needing the rabbit, not the squirrel.\n\nSo, does the tiger needing the rabbit depend on whether it's eating it? Or is it a separate need?\n\nI think the key is that if someone is green, they eat the rabbit. So the tiger, being green, must eat the rabbit. Therefore, the tiger needs the rabbit because it's eating it. So the statement is true.\n\nWait, but point 20 says if the tiger needs the rabbit, then the rabbit is green. But we don't know if the rabbit is green. So if the tiger needs the rabbit, the rabbit must be green. But if the rabbit isn't green, then the tiger doesn't need the rabbit.\n\nBut from point 17, the tiger is green, so it must eat the rabbit, which implies it needs the rabbit. But if the rabbit isn't green, that's a contradiction. So maybe the rabbit is green, making the statement true.\