hite. So, is it possible that Harry is white? If he is white, then he's cold, and thus from statement 12, since he's rough, he must be round. But we don't know if he's round. However, from statement 15, he's rough because he's furry and green. So, he's rough, but we don't know if he's round.\n\nWait, but statement 12 says all cold, rough people are round. So if Harry is cold (which he would be if he's white), and he's rough, then he must be round. But we don't know if he's round yet. So, is there any way to determine if he's round?\n\nAlternatively, let's look at statement 16: All round, green people are cold. So if Harry is round and green, he must be cold. But we don't know if he's round yet.\n\nWait, but from statement 15, all furry, green people are rough. So Harry is furry and green, so he's rough. So Harry is rough. Now, if Harry is rough and green, then from statement 16, he must be cold. So, if Harry is rough and green, he's cold. But from statement 11, all cold people are white, so if he's cold, he's white. So, if Harry is rough and green, he's cold and thus white.\n\nBut wait, that would mean Harry is white. But we don't know if he's round yet. So, if Harry is white, he's cold, and thus from statement 12, since he's rough, he must be round. So, Harry would have to be round if he's white.\n\nBut do we have any information about Harry being round? No, we don't. So, is there a way to determine if Harry is round or not?\n\nWait, let's see. From statement 15, Harry is rough because he's furry and green. So, he's rough. Now, if he's rough and green, he must be cold (from statement 16). And if he's cold, he's white (from statement 11). So, if Harry is rough and green, he's white. But we don't know if he's round yet.\n\nBut from statement 12, all cold, rough people are round. So, if Harry is cold and rough, he must be round. So, if Harry is white, he's cold, rough, and thus round. But we don't know if he's round yet.\n\nWait, but we don't have any information abou