ck again. Point 8 says being young implies being smart, but Bob is smart, so he could be young or not. However, point 11 says being smart implies being green. So Bob is green. But does being green and smart make someone young? Point 10 says if someone is green and young, they are white. But Bob is smart and possibly green, but we don't know if he's young. So, if Bob is green and young, he would be white, but we don't know if he's white. But point 7 says Gary is white, but that doesn't affect Bob.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Bob is not green because he's not mentioned as such, but point 11 says if someone is smart, they are green. So Bob, being smart, must be green. But does being green make someone young? Not necessarily. So Bob could be green and not young, or green and young. But since being young implies being smart, and Bob is smart, he could be young or not. However, the question is whether he is young.\n\nWait, but let's think about the implications again. If someone is young, they are smart (point 8). Bob is smart, so he could be young or not. But is there any other information that ties Bob's age? The only other person mentioned as young is Fiona (point 4), and she's smart. But Bob's smartness doesn't necessarily tie him to being young or not.\n\nWait, but point 11 says if someone is smart, they are green. So Bob is green. Now, if someone is green and young, they are white (point 10). But Bob is smart and possibly green, but we don't know if he's young. So, if Bob were young, he would have to be white. But we don't know if he's white. Alternatively, if he's not young, he's green but not white. But since we don't have any information about Bob's color beyond what's inferred, we can't determine if he's young or not.\n\nWait, but let me check again. The only people we know for sure are Fiona (young, smart), Gary (nice, smart, white). Bob is round and smart. So, Bob is smart, so he's green (point 11). Now, if Bob is green and young, he would be white (point 10). But