doesn't directly relate to age.\n\nSo, Bob is smart and green, but we don't know if he's young or not. Therefore, the statement \"Bob is young\" is unknown based on the given information.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I'm not missing anything. Is there any rule that would link Bob's age directly? I don't think so. All the rules either talk about implications of being young, smart, green, white, quiet, or nice. There's no rule that directly states whether Bob is young or not. So, without more information, we can't determine if he's young or not.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be unknown.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C) Unknown\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. Bob is round and smart.\n2. Being smart (point 2) implies being green (point 11).\n3. Being young implies being smart (point 8), but being smart doesn't necessarily mean being young.\n4. There's no direct information about Bob's age; he could be young or not.\n5. Without additional information linking Bob's age, the statement's truth cannot be determined.\n\nAnswer: C) Unknown",
