ething chases the cat, then the cat eats the bald eagle. So, if the bald eagle chases the cat, the cat would eat the bald eagle. But statement 1 says the bald eagle chases the squirrel, not the cat. So, the bald eagle isn't chasing the cat, so statement 9 doesn't apply here.\n\nStatement 11: If something sees the bald eagle and does not chase the cat, then the cat chases the bald eagle. So, if the cat sees the bald eagle and doesn't chase the cat (which it can't chase itself), then the cat would chase the bald eagle. But statement 5 says the cat does not see the bald eagle, so this condition isn't met. Therefore, statement 11 doesn't apply either.\n\nStatement 7: The tiger chases the squirrel. So, the tiger is chasing the squirrel. From statement 13, if the tiger chases the squirrel, then the tiger eats the tiger? Wait, that can't be right. Wait, no, statement 13 says if something chases the squirrel, then it eats the tiger. So, the tiger chasing the squirrel would mean the tiger eats the tiger? That doesn't make sense. Maybe I'm misunderstanding.\n\nWait, no, statement 13 is: If something chases the squirrel, then it eats the tiger. So, the tiger is something that chases the squirrel, so the tiger eats the tiger. That seems like a loop, which might not be possible. Maybe the tiger can't eat itself, so perhaps this leads to a contradiction, meaning the tiger doesn't chase the squirrel. But statement 7 says the tiger does chase the squirrel, so maybe this is a problem.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake. Let me check statement 13 again. It says if something chases the squirrel, then it eats the tiger. So, the tiger is something that chases the squirrel, so the tiger eats the tiger. That's a bit odd, but maybe it's a way to set up a contradiction. If the tiger eats itself, that's impossible, so perhaps the tiger doesn't chase the squirrel. But statement 7 says the tiger does chase the squirrel, so this seems contradictory. Maybe this means that the tiger can't chase the squirrel, which would mean statement 7 is false. But I'm not sure if that's the case.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger chasing the squirrel leads to the tiger eating the tiger, which is impossible, so the tiger can't chase the squirrel, making statement 7 false. But that would mean the tiger doesn't chase the squirrel, which contradicts statement 7. So, perhaps this is a paradox, but maybe it's just a way to set up the scenario.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger chasing the squirrel leads to the tiger eating the tiger, which is impossible, so the tiger can't chase the squirrel, making statement 7 false. But that would mean the tiger doesn't chase the squirrel, which contradicts statement 7. So, perhaps the tiger doesn't chase the squirrel, making statement 7 false. But I'm not sure if that's the case.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating this. Let's try to focus on the question: Does the bald eagle eat the squirrel? From statement 1, the bald eagle chases the squirrel, but that doesn't necessarily mean it eats it. So, we need to see if there's any information that leads us to conclude whether the bald eagle eats the squirrel or not.\n\nLooking at statement 14: If something eats the squirrel, then it eats the bald eagle. So, if the squirrel is eaten by something, that something also eats the bald eagle. But we don't know if the squirrel is eaten by anything.\n\nStatement 15: If the squirrel sees the bald eagle and the cat does not chase the squirrel, then the cat does not eat the squirrel. But we don't know if the squirrel sees the bald eagle. Statement 5 says the cat does not see the bald eagle, but that's about the cat, not the squirrel.\n\nStatement 10: If the cat sees the squirrel and the cat is not rough, then the squirrel is big. But the cat is rough (statement 3), so the condition \"the cat is not rough\" is false, so statement 10 doesn't apply.\n\nStatement 2: The cat eats the squirrel. So, the cat is eating the squirrel. From statement 15, if the squirrel sees the bald eagle and the cat does not chase the squirrel, then the cat does not eat the squirrel. But in our case, the cat is eating the squirrel (statement 2), so the condition in statement 15 is not met because the cat is eating the squirrel, which would mean the cat does eat the squirrel, so the conclusion of statement 15 is false. Therefore, statement 15's condition isn't met, so it doesn't affect our situation.\n\nWait, no, statement 15 is an implication: If A and B, then C. So, if A and B are true, then C must be true. But if A and B are not both true, then the implication doesn't tell us anything. So, since the cat is eating the squirrel (statement 2), which would mean that the condition in statement 15 isn't met, so we can't conclude anything from statement 15.\n\nNow, going back to statement 9: If something chases the cat, then the cat eats the bald eagle. But we don't know if something is chasing the cat. Statement 5 says the cat does not see the bald eagle, but that's about the cat's perception, not about someone chasing the cat.\n\nStatement 11: If something sees the bald eagle and does not chase the cat, then the cat chases the bald eagle. But the cat does not see the bald eagle (statement 5), so the condition is met: something (the cat) sees the bald eagle? Wait, no, statement 5 says the cat does not see the bald eagle, so the condition in statement 11 is not met. Therefore, statement 11 doesn't apply.\n\nWait, statement 11 says: If something sees the bald eagle and does not chase the cat, then the cat chases the bald eagle. But the cat does not see the bald eagle (statement 5), so the condition is not met, so statement 11 doesn't apply.\n\nSo, the cat is eating the squirrel (statement 2), and the cat is young (statement 4). The cat does not see the bald eagle (statement 5). The tiger chases the squirrel (statement 7), but that leads to a contradiction because the tiger would eat itself (statement 13), which is impossible, so perhaps the tiger doesn