ut does that mean it likes it? The context doesn't specify that chasing implies liking. So just because the rabbit chases the mouse doesn't necessarily mean it likes it. So without any other information, we can't conclude that the rabbit likes the mouse.\n\nBut wait, statement 14 could be used if the cat eats the cat and chases the rabbit, but the cat isn't eating itself because the dog is eating the cat. So statement 14 doesn't apply.\n\nTherefore, the rabbit chasing the mouse doesn't lead to the rabbit liking the mouse because the condition in statement 14 isn't met. So the statement \"The rabbit likes the mouse\" is unknown because we don't have enough information to confirm it.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought it was true, but now I'm seeing it's unknown. I'm confused.\n\nLet me try to summarize:\n\n- The cat chases the rabbit (1).\n- The cat likes the mouse (2).\n- The dog is blue (3).\n- The dog likes the cat (5) and the mouse (6).\n- The mouse is cold (7) and doesn't like the cat (8).\n- The rabbit chases the mouse (9) and is nice (10).\n- If someone eats the cat and chases the rabbit, then the rabbit likes the mouse (14).\n- If someone likes the mouse, they are blue (13).\n- If someone eats the rabbit, they like the rabbit (17).\n- If someone is blue, they eat the cat (18).\n\nFrom 18, the dog is blue, so the dog eats the cat. From 5, the dog likes the cat, which is consistent.\n\nFrom 16, the cat chases the dog because it chases the rabbit.\n\nFrom 14, for the rabbit to like the mouse, the condition is that someone eats the cat and chases the rabbit. The dog eats the cat, but does the dog chase the rabbit? From 16, the cat chases the dog, but that doesn't mean the dog chases the cat. So the dog might not be chasing the rabbit. Therefore, statement 14 doesn't apply.\n\nSo, without statement 14 being applicable, we can't conclude that the rabbit likes the mouse. Therefore, the statement is unknown.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought it was true because of a possible misinterpretation, but now I'm seeing it's unknown. So I think the correct answer is that the statement is unknown because we don't have enough information to confirm it.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C) Unknown\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. The cat chases the rabbit (1).\n2. The cat likes the mouse (2).\n3. The dog is blue (3).\n4. The dog likes the mouse (6).\n5. The mouse is cold (7).\n6. The mouse does not like the cat (8).\n7. The rabbit chases the mouse (9).\n8. The rabbit is nice (10).\n9. If someone eats the cat and chases the rabbit, then the rabbit likes the mouse (14).\n10. The dog is blue, so it eats the cat (18).\n11. The dog likes the cat (5) and the mouse (6).\n12. The cat chases the dog because it chases the rabbit (16).\n13. If someone likes the mouse, they are blue (13).\n14. The rabbit chasing the mouse doesn't necessarily mean it likes it because statement 14 requires the mouse to be eaten by someone who also chases the rabbit. However, the dog eats the cat but doesn't chase the rabbit, so statement 14 doesn't apply.\n15. Without statement 14 being applicable, we can't conclude the rabbit likes the mouse.\n\nAnswer: C) Unknown",
