can't be big. So Bob is not big.\n\nBut does that affect whether he's rough? Not directly. So Bob is kind, nice, round, not red, and not big. Now, looking back at point 12: All kind, red things are rough. Since Bob is kind but not red, he doesn't fall under this category. So he isn't necessarily rough.\n\nWait, but could there be another rule that makes him rough? Let me check point 14 again: If something is rough, then it's round. But that doesn't imply that all round things are rough, only that rough things are round. So being round doesn't make him rough unless there's another condition.\n\nWait, point 11 says all round things are nice. Bob is round and nice, which fits, but that doesn't make him rough. So I think the key here is that since Bob is kind and not red, the rule about kind and red things being rough doesn't apply to him. Therefore, Bob isn't rough.\n\nSo the statement \"Bob is rough\" would be false.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B) False\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. **Identify Bob's Attributes**: Bob is kind, nice, and round (from points 3,4,7).\n\n2. **Apply Rule 12**: Since Bob is kind but not red, he doesn't fall under the category of kind and red things, which are rough. Therefore, Bob isn't rough.\n\n3. **Conclusion**: The statement \"Bob is rough\" is false.\n\nThe correct option is: B) False",
