ear is kind. But the mouse being big doesn't make it kind unless it's visited by something.\n\nWait, the mouse is big because it doesn't eat the tiger. But the mouse being big doesn't make it kind unless it's visited by something. So, the mouse is big, but we don't know if it's visited by anyone else.\n\nSo, I think the bear likes the mouse is not necessarily true. The bear likes the tiger, which makes it visit the mouse, making it kind, but that doesn't mean the bear likes the mouse. So, the answer might be unknown.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The bear likes the tiger, which makes it visit the mouse, making it kind. But does the bear liking the mouse have any connection? I don't think so. So, maybe the answer is unknown.\n\nWait, but the bear is kind because it visited the mouse, and the bear is blue. The only cold things are the bald eagle and the tiger. So, the bear is not cold, so it's not big. But that doesn't affect whether it likes the mouse.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bear likes the mouse because the mouse is big, but I don't see a rule that connects the mouse's size to the bear's liking.\n\nSo, I think the answer is unknown because there's no direct rule connecting the bear liking the mouse. The bear likes the tiger, which makes it visit the mouse, making it kind, but that doesn't necessarily mean the bear likes the mouse.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B) False\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. The bear eats the bald eagle (bear \u2192 eats bald eagle).\n2. The bald eagle is cold and does not like the bear.\n3. The bald eagle likes the tiger (bald eagle \u2192 likes tiger).\n4. The tiger is cold and likes the bear (tiger \u2192 likes bear).\n5. The mouse likes the tiger (mouse \u2192 likes tiger).\n6. The tiger eats the mouse (tiger \u2192 eats mouse).\n7. The mouse is big because it does not eat the tiger (mouse \u2192 big).\n8. The bear visits the mouse because it is kind (bear \u2192 visits mouse).\n9. The bear is kind because it visited the